    {
      "id": "02",
      "title": "Circular imports",
      "question": "a imports b and b imports a. Why does it fail with AttributeError instead of looping forever?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question two. Module a imports b, and module b imports a. Why does that fail with an attribute error, instead of looping forever? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "<em>a</em> imports <em>b</em>, <em>b</em> imports <em>a</em>: why AttributeError, not a loop?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "compare",
          "say": "Here is the classic cycle. a imports b at the top, before defining X. And b imports a, then reads a dot X.",
          "props": {
            "title": "The classic <em>cycle</em>",
            "lang": "python",
            "left": {
              "at": "top",
              "label": "a.py",
              "title": "imports b first",
              "code": "# a.py\nimport b\nX = 42"
            },
            "right": {
              "at": "reads",
              "label": "b.py",
              "title": "reads a.X",
              "tone": "bad",
              "code": "# b.py\nimport a\nprint(a.X)   # AttributeError"
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "graph",
          "say": "Import a. Python caches an empty a first, then starts running it. a's first line imports b, so b is cached, and starts running. b's first line imports a: that's a cache hit, so there's no infinite loop. But it returns the partially initialised a, whose X has not been assigned yet. So b's print raises an attribute error.",
          "props": {
            "title": "A cache hit on a <em>half-built</em> module",
            "nodes": [
              {
                "id": "A",
                "label": "a.py",
                "x": 240,
                "y": 470,
                "w": 200,
                "h": 64
              },
              {
                "id": "B",
                "label": "b.py",
                "x": 880,
                "y": 470,
                "w": 200,
                "h": 64
              }
            ],
            "edges": [],
            "panels": [
              {
                "key": "mods",
                "label": "sys.modules",
                "type": "list",
                "rows": 1,
                "initial": []
              },
              {
                "key": "ax",
                "label": "a.X",
                "type": "list",
                "initial": [
                  "not yet defined"
                ]
              }
            ],
            "steps": [
              {
                "at": "caches",
                "nodes": {
                  "A": "active"
                },
                "panels": {
                  "mods": [
                    "a → <partial>"
                  ]
                },
                "note": "a is cached, then starts running"
              },
              {
                "at": "first#2",
                "add": [
                  {
                    "from": "A",
                    "to": "B",
                    "w": "import b"
                  }
                ],
                "nodes": {
                  "A": "queued",
                  "B": "active"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "A-B": "relax"
                },
                "panels": {
                  "mods": [
                    "a → <partial>",
                    "b → <partial>"
                  ]
                },
                "note": "a pauses at line 1: import b"
              },
              {
                "at": "hit",
                "add": [
                  {
                    "from": "B",
                    "to": "A",
                    "w": "import a"
                  }
                ],
                "edges": {
                  "A-B": "base",
                  "B-A": "relax"
                },
                "note": "b imports a: cache hit, no loop"
              },
              {
                "at": "partially",
                "nodes": {
                  "A": "dim"
                },
                "note": "…but a is half-built: X = 42 hasn't run"
              },
              {
                "at": "raises",
                "nodes": {
                  "B": "queued"
                },
                "edges": {
                  "B-A": "dim"
                },
                "note": "print(a.X) → AttributeError"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Fixes. Import inside the function that needs it, so it runs later. Import the module, not the name, and read a dot X at call time. Move the shared pieces into a third module that both import. And for imports needed only by type hints, guard them with typing's TYPE CHECKING flag.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Breaking the <em>cycle</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "inside",
                "label": "Defer",
                "title": "Import in the function",
                "body": "Runs after both modules finish loading.",
                "code": "def f(): import a"
              },
              {
                "at": "name",
                "label": "Late binding",
                "title": "import a, use a.X later",
                "body": "Not from a import X at the top.",
                "code": "a.X at call time"
              },
              {
                "at": "third",
                "label": "Restructure",
                "title": "Shared third module",
                "body": "Both import it; no cycle.",
                "code": "common.py"
              },
              {
                "at": "hints",
                "label": "Types only",
                "title": "TYPE_CHECKING",
                "body": "Imports skipped at runtime.",
                "code": "if TYPE_CHECKING:"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "03",
      "title": "Packages and reload",
      "question": "What is a package, how do relative imports work, and what does reload really do?",
      "scenes": [
        {
          "type": "question",
          "say": "Question three. What makes a directory a package, how do relative imports work, and what does reload really do? Pause and answer out loud.",
          "pauseAfter": 3,
          "props": {
            "question": "Packages, <em>relative</em> imports, and <em>reload</em>?"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "A directory with dunder init dot py is a regular package, and importing it runs that file. Since Python three point three, a directory without one still imports, as a namespace package. Inside a package, a leading dot means relative to the current package, and two dots means its parent. And importlib reload re-executes a module into the same module object; but anything that already grabbed an old function or class keeps the old one.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Packages and <em>reload</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "regular",
                "label": "__init__.py",
                "title": "Regular package",
                "body": "Importing the package runs it.",
                "code": "import mypkg"
              },
              {
                "at": "namespace",
                "label": "No __init__.py",
                "title": "Namespace package",
                "body": "Still importable since Python 3.3.",
                "code": "PEP 420"
              },
              {
                "at": "dot",
                "label": "Relative",
                "title": ". and ..",
                "body": "Current package, then its parent.",
                "code": "from ..sub import h"
              },
              {
                "at": "reload",
                "label": "reload()",
                "title": "Re-run in place",
                "body": "Old references keep the old objects.",
                "code": "importlib.reload"
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "recap",
          "say": "To recap. Import checks sys modules first, and runs module code only once. On a miss, the module is cached before it executes, which is why circular imports return half-built modules. Fix cycles by deferring or restructuring imports. And reload re-runs a module, without updating references others already hold.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Imports, <em>in four lines</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "once",
                "text": "sys.modules first; module code runs once.",
                "code": "cache"
              },
              {
                "at": "half-built",
                "text": "Cached before executing → partial modules in cycles.",
                "code": "AttributeError"
              },
              {
                "at": "deferring",
                "text": "Fix cycles: defer, late-bind, restructure.",
                "code": "import inside f()"
              },
              {
                "at": "reload",
                "text": "reload re-runs; old references stay old.",
                "code": "importlib.reload"
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
